The ATmega2560 register names are from `<avr/io.h>`. Set the desired waveform/counting modes independently. The clock-select commands preserve the other control bits.

```cpp
#include <avr/io.h>

// (i) Toggle the PC3 output latch.
DDRC |= _BV(PC3);
PORTC ^= _BV(PC3);

// (ii) Timer5, internal clock divided by 64: CS52:0 = 011.
TCCR5B = (TCCR5B & ~(_BV(CS52) | _BV(CS51) | _BV(CS50)))
       | _BV(CS51) | _BV(CS50);

// (iii) Timer1, external rising edges on T1 (PD6): CS12:0 = 111.
DDRD &= ~_BV(PD6);
TCCR1B |= _BV(CS12) | _BV(CS11) | _BV(CS10);

// (iv) Internal 2.56 V reference: REFS1:0 = 11.
ADMUX |= _BV(REFS1) | _BV(REFS0);

// (v) Left-adjust the ADC result.
ADMUX |= _BV(ADLAR);
```

Use the chip's actual T1 pin. `ICES1` selects the separate input-capture edge. For the internal reference, use the AREF bypass capacitor and leave any external reference voltage disconnected. Allow the reference to settle. [Microchip ATmega2560 datasheet, ADMUX register](https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/devicedoc/atmega640-1280-1281-2560-2561-datasheet-ds40002211a.pdf#page=281).

For a fixed instruction sequence, divide the sum of instruction cycles by the CPU frequency. For a measurement in a sketch, subtract the `micros()` timestamp taken before the group from the timestamp taken after it. Print after the second timestamp so the serial transmission does not enter the measurement.

For example, `ldi` takes one cycle, each `dec` takes one, and `brne` takes two cycles when taken and one otherwise. The group below takes $1+200+199\times2+1=600$ cycles, or $37.5\,\mu\mathrm{s}$ at 16 MHz.

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

void setup() {
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    const uint32_t startUs = micros();
    asm volatile (
        "ldi r18, 200\n\t"
        "1: dec r18\n\t"
        "brne 1b\n\t"
        ::: "r18", "cc"
    );
    const uint32_t elapsedUs = micros() - startUs;
    Serial.print(F("Measured time (us): "));
    Serial.println(elapsedUs);
    delay(1000);
}
```

The 16 MHz Mega's standard `micros()` resolution is $4\,\mu\mathrm{s}$. The result includes timer-read overhead and any interrupt during the interval. Measuring several repetitions improves the average for a short sequence. This adapts the lecture's ADC timing measurement.

Assume active-high outputs, a 5 V Mega 2560 and a common ground. Use the lecture's direct-source connection for the low-current LED and low-side NPN driver for the higher-current LED. PB4 is D10 and PB5 is D11.

```text
PB4 / D10 ── Rlow ──── low-current LED ── GND

 +5 V ────── Rhigh ─── high-current LED ── collector
PB5 / D11 ── Rbase ─── base                 NPN
                                          emitter ── GND
```

Size the series resistors for the actual forward voltages and desired currents. For example, $V_F\approx2\,\mathrm V$ and 1 mA gives $R_\mathrm{low}\approx3\,\mathrm{k\Omega}$. For 10 mA, with a saturated transistor dropping about 0.2 V, $R_\mathrm{high}\approx280\,\Omega$ (300 or 330 Ω is suitable for this example). A 3.9 kΩ base resistor supplies about 1.1 mA at $V_{BE}\approx0.7\,\mathrm V$. Verify the selected device's drive and saturation ratings. The base resistor limits GPIO current.

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

constexpr uint8_t lowLed = _BV(PB4);
constexpr uint8_t highLed = _BV(PB5);
constexpr uint8_t ledMask = lowLed | highLed;

void setup() {
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
    DDRB |= ledMask;
}

void loop() {
    PORTB = (PORTB & ~ledMask) | lowLed;
    delay(500);
    PORTB = (PORTB & ~ledMask) | highLed;
    delay(500);
}
```

Each update sets one LED bit and clears the other without changing the remaining port bits.

**Assumptions.** A 16 MHz Mega 2560 with Timer4 reserved for this task. Use non-inverting PWM on **OC4A (PH3, D6)**. Select phase-and-frequency-correct PWM, mode 8, where `ICR4` is TOP, and divide the clock by 64.

The counter goes from BOTTOM to TOP and back. OC4A clears on the upward compare and sets on the downward compare. Thus

$$
f_{\mathrm{PWM}}=\frac{16\,000\,000}{2\cdot64\cdot\mathrm{ICR4}}.
$$

`ICR4 = 12500` gives 10 Hz. `OCR4A = 10000` gives a duty ratio of $10000/12500=0.8$. The steady-state high and low times are 80 ms and 20 ms.

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

void setup() {
    TCCR4B = 0;  // Stop the timer.
    TCCR4A = 0;
    TIMSK4 = 0;  // Hardware generates the waveform.
    PORTH &= ~_BV(PH3);
    DDRH |= _BV(PH3);
    TCNT4 = 0;

    TCCR4B = _BV(WGM43);  // Mode 8, clock still stopped.
    ICR4 = 12500;
    OCR4A = 10000;
    TCCR4A = _BV(COM4A1);
    TCCR4B = _BV(WGM43) | _BV(CS41) | _BV(CS40);
}

void loop() {}
```

The notes give the timer and prescaler setup. The dual-slope output mode is an extension from the [ATmega2560 datasheet, §17.9.5](https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/devicedoc/atmega640-1280-1281-2560-2561-datasheet-ds40002211a.pdf#page=150).

Use **ADC2 (PF2/A2)**, 5 V AVCC as the reference, the usual AREF bypass capacitor and a shared ground. Keep the analog input between ground and AVCC. A divider of 64 gives $16\,\mathrm{MHz}/64=250\,\mathrm{kHz}$, so `ADPS2:0=110`.

Set `ADLAR=1`, so `ADCH` holds result bits 9–2 for the eight-bit `PORTC`. The requested clock is above the recommended range for maximum 10-bit resolution. [ATmega2560 datasheet, ADC prescaling and conversion timing](https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/devicedoc/atmega640-1280-1281-2560-2561-datasheet-ds40002211a.pdf#page=271).

```cpp
#include <Arduino.h>

constexpr uint8_t adcControl = _BV(ADEN) | _BV(ADPS2) | _BV(ADPS1);

void setup() {
    PORTC = 0;
    DDRC = 0xFF;
    DDRF &= ~_BV(PF2);
    PORTF &= ~_BV(PF2);
    DIDR0 |= _BV(ADC2D);

    ADCSRB = 0;  // MUX5 = 0, software triggering.
    ADMUX = _BV(REFS0) | _BV(ADLAR) | _BV(MUX1);
    ADCSRA = adcControl | _BV(ADIF);  // Clear any old completion flag.
}

void loop() {
    ADCSRA = adcControl | _BV(ADSC);
    while (!(ADCSRA & _BV(ADIF))) {}
    PORTC = ADCH;
    ADCSRA = adcControl | _BV(ADIF);  // Clear ADIF by writing one.
}
```

